WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:04.820
Leah: Risk is often defined as an
event or activity that could have

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:06.870
a positive or negative outcome.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:11.100
When doing something that involves
risk we often have to weigh up if the

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.520
potential reward is worth the risk!

00:00:14.099 --> 00:00:17.819
Just think of some of those risky
activities like ‘skydiving’ or

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:21.880
‘swimming with sharks’ to something
as simple as ‘crossing the road’!

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:26.770
In all cases we have to identify
the risks and minimise them.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.889
This is risk management!

00:00:28.889 --> 00:00:31.517
Chris: So, what do we
mean by risk management?

00:00:32.297 --> 00:00:36.747
In business risk management can be
defined as the process used to ‘identify,

00:00:36.977 --> 00:00:41.427
assess and control threats to the
organisations financial stability’.

00:00:42.277 --> 00:00:47.227
In coding we are referring to a ‘potential
situation arising that can have a

00:00:47.237 --> 00:00:50.407
direct impact on a projects progress’.

00:00:51.517 --> 00:00:55.467
Leah: Some potential risks may seem
rather extreme such as a ‘global

00:00:55.477 --> 00:00:57.717
pandemic’, ‘terrorist attack’ or ‘war’!

00:00:58.147 --> 00:01:02.337
however in today’s world these are
becoming more plausible as we all know!

00:01:02.987 --> 00:01:08.017
But on the whole, all projects will have
more realistic risks attached such as,

00:01:08.367 --> 00:01:12.847
project failure, too much time spent
on the project, overspend on budget

00:01:12.937 --> 00:01:15.127
or possibly a poor-quality product.

00:01:16.217 --> 00:01:20.047
Chris: The role of the project manager is
to identify the major risk factors that

00:01:20.047 --> 00:01:25.397
may affect the project, evaluate these,
and prioritise them, and then plan how

00:01:25.397 --> 00:01:28.377
these risks can be managed or mitigated.

00:01:29.057 --> 00:01:33.377
They are also responsible for reviewing
and monitoring for any new risks that

00:01:33.377 --> 00:01:36.407
might arise and updating all stakeholders.

00:01:37.477 --> 00:01:41.997
Leah: Risks can be split up into two
types, Pure risks and Business risks.

00:01:42.967 --> 00:01:47.487
Pure risks are extreme and often involve
the ultimate use of the software in

00:01:47.577 --> 00:01:52.297
high-risk environments where failure of
the software may have dire consequences.

00:01:52.847 --> 00:01:56.227
These don’t directly affect the
software development but have to be

00:01:56.227 --> 00:01:58.347
considered regarding its deployment.

00:01:59.257 --> 00:02:03.337
Chris: Business risks are more likely
to be encountered and often involve

00:02:03.337 --> 00:02:06.727
risk associated with financial
aspects such as cost cutting

00:02:06.727 --> 00:02:08.857
exercises and a lack of vision.

00:02:09.637 --> 00:02:14.017
Every project has risks, but
software projects are special.

00:02:14.477 --> 00:02:18.747
So, what are the potential risks that
could affect a projects delivery?

00:02:19.397 --> 00:02:21.127
Let’s take a look at the most common.

00:02:22.517 --> 00:02:23.647
Leah: Experience

00:02:23.807 --> 00:02:28.067
A business risk may involve using less
experienced developers to save money.

00:02:28.387 --> 00:02:32.427
This lack of experience could affect the
quality and deadline for the project.

00:02:32.917 --> 00:02:37.507
However, the developer may rise to the
challenge and proves to be an asset and of

00:02:37.507 --> 00:02:40.227
greater value in this and future projects.

00:02:40.877 --> 00:02:42.377
Chris: Time and costs

00:02:43.127 --> 00:02:45.262
Time estimates need to be realistic.

00:02:45.662 --> 00:02:48.882
If a project team struggles to
meet the deadlines, quality could

00:02:48.882 --> 00:02:53.192
suffer, which means work may need
to be redone and more delays.

00:02:54.002 --> 00:02:57.572
In software development the
largest expense is usually

00:02:57.572 --> 00:02:59.202
tied to the developers’ time.

00:02:59.642 --> 00:03:03.502
So, If the time estimates are out
then so will be the cost estimates.

00:03:04.222 --> 00:03:08.552
As costs pile up, stakeholders,
primarily management, may start

00:03:08.552 --> 00:03:12.602
to lose faith, putting pressure
on for completion, resulting in

00:03:12.602 --> 00:03:15.272
errors, lost time, and lost money!

00:03:15.922 --> 00:03:16.382
Leah: Scope

00:03:16.412 --> 00:03:19.552
Scope is the agreed body of work
that the project will capture.

00:03:20.002 --> 00:03:24.202
‘Scope creep’, also called ‘mission
creep’, can occur when stakeholders

00:03:24.202 --> 00:03:29.132
begin to sneak in changes that circumvent
the identified change request system

00:03:29.132 --> 00:03:31.262
and time and cost are affected.

00:03:31.872 --> 00:03:35.602
Scope changes may also stem from
the project team not having a clear

00:03:35.632 --> 00:03:39.502
understanding of what’s considered
‘in scope’ and what’s ‘out of scope’.

00:03:40.272 --> 00:03:45.152
Scope changes should be avoided unless
absolutely necessary but should always be

00:03:45.152 --> 00:03:47.792
reflected in the time and cost estimates.

00:03:48.322 --> 00:03:49.202
Chris: Feasibility

00:03:50.012 --> 00:03:54.849
some projects are unrealistic in
regard to scope, schedule, and budget.

00:03:55.469 --> 00:03:58.509
It’s the project manager’s job
to identify this unfeasibility

00:03:58.509 --> 00:04:01.369
or face the consequences.

00:04:01.839 --> 00:04:02.589
Leah: Quality

00:04:03.589 --> 00:04:06.519
what good is a project if it’s
completed on time and on budget

00:04:06.569 --> 00:04:09.929
but the software is full of bugs,
errors, and crashes all the time?

00:04:10.579 --> 00:04:12.459
Chris: Stakeholder expectations.

00:04:14.389 --> 00:04:17.179
title on screen

00:04:17.179 --> 00:04:17.335
You must remember to maintain
communication channels.

00:04:17.335 --> 00:04:19.289
Failure to ensure that the
project is moving in alignment

00:04:19.309 --> 00:04:23.429
with stakeholder expectations can
risk not meeting their vision.

00:04:24.049 --> 00:04:25.309
Leah: Human Resources

00:04:25.819 --> 00:04:29.269
Knowledgeable technical resources
are always in great demand.

00:04:29.649 --> 00:04:33.379
You may run the risk of your top
developers being recruited by other

00:04:33.379 --> 00:04:36.619
firms therefore it’s important
to consider employee retention

00:04:36.619 --> 00:04:38.529
strategies to mitigate this.

00:04:39.549 --> 00:04:40.969
Technical accuracy.

00:04:41.869 --> 00:04:44.169
Chris: If your project team
doesn’t have the skills to deliver

00:04:44.199 --> 00:04:46.209
the project this is a risk.

00:04:46.739 --> 00:04:49.999
The project team must be trained
and developed in order to reach

00:04:49.999 --> 00:04:53.889
the project conclusion that you
and the stakeholder are expecting.

00:04:54.109 --> 00:04:57.979
Leah: As well as these potential risks
there is also the risk that, if your

00:04:57.989 --> 00:05:02.379
project is something that has never been
attempted before, you risk suffering

00:05:02.379 --> 00:05:04.989
from the first-time, first-use penalty.

00:05:05.639 --> 00:05:09.289
This penalty basically means that
because it’s never been done before

00:05:09.609 --> 00:05:13.759
there is a higher risk of facing problems
you couldn’t possibly anticipate.

